5.1 Level Layout Pattern
  Chosen pattern: Linear.
  A linear layout suits the first level because Dead Player Walking must teach movement, attack, dodge/repositioning, and the previous-self mechanic in a controlled order. It allows one new demand to be introduced at a time, includes a short recovery space, and then combines familiar mechanics in a final encounter. An open or hub-and-spoke layout would offer more route freedom but reduce control over the teaching order and difficulty curve.
  5.2 Annotated Level Map
  The sequence below is the proposed first-level map. It identifies the player start, objective, major obstacles, and rising/resting pacing beats.
  Zone / map flow
  Annotation and gameplay purpose
  1. START — P
  Safe start area with no active enemies; player tests movement.
  ↓ 2. MOVEMENT SPACE
  Walls or a narrow passage are obstacles. Their placement encourages repositioning.
  ↓ 3. BASIC COMBAT — E
  One or two weak enemies. RISING BEAT: player must attack to clear the route.
  ↓ 4. DODGE ARENA — E + !
  Enemy attack pattern or visible hazard. RISING BEAT: player must dodge or reposition.
  ↓ 5. RECOVERY ROOM — R
  No active enemies. RESTING BEAT: short pause before the new threat.
  ↓ 6. PREVIOUS-SELF ARENA — S
  One recorded previous self. RISING BEAT: introduce the signature mechanic in a readable space.
  ↓ 7. FINAL ARENA — E + S
  Ordinary enemy/enemies plus one previous self. PEAK BEAT: combine learned mechanics.
  ↓ 8. EXIT / OBJECTIVE — *
  Reach the exit to complete the level; pressure releases after the peak encounter.


Legend: P = player start; E = ordinary enemy; ! = hazard/dodge cue; R = recovery/rest; S = previous self; * = objective.
This is a design proposal, not a claim that all zones already exist in the Godot prototype. Implement and test zones incrementally.

